  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nSurface ridge of high pressure extending from the New England coast \nwill exit the region today as a cold front approaches from the Upper \nGreat Lakes region. Ahead of the cold front, southerly flow \nwill warm high temperatures into the mid to upper 70s, roughly \n10-15 degrees above normal for late October. A few sites have \nthe potential to approach or reach record warm high \ntemperatures for today. The cold front will arrive in Northwest \nOhio this afternoon and gradually slide southeast across the \nforecast area through late tonight. Scattered showers will be \npossible along the front with precipitation chances remaining \nbetween 40-50% with locally higher chances (50-70%) across \nportions of Northeast Ohio and Northwest Pennsylvania where \nbetter upper level support is present. Cannot rule out a rumble \nof thunder across Northwest Ohio late afternoon/early evening as\nMLCAPE values of 200-400 J/kg are forecast. \n\nDry weather returns Friday and Saturday as another area of high\npressure builds across the region. Slightly cooler, albeit \nstill above normal, with highs in the 60s Friday and Saturday \nbehind the cold front. Chilly overnight lows Friday night in the\n40s for most and the low to mid 30s in the higher terrain of \nNorthwest Pennsylvania. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThere remains some discrepancy between medium/long range models \nregarding rain chances Sunday night into Monday. Overall, the \nforecast is trending drier for that time frame as the aforementioned \nsurface high centered over the region may inhibit rainfall. The high \npushes to the east early next week as a broad upper trough digs into \nthe central and eastern CONUS. Models seem to be in a bit better \nagreement with this system bringing appreciable rainfall to the \nregion sometime middle of next week.",
